WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 150: Chester A. Arthur and the Tiffany Glass: The Secret Illness and the Quiet Farewell

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.120
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our portrait of Chester A.

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.040
[Rebecca] Arthur by entering the private, shadowed quarters of his White House.

5
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:26.960
[Rebecca] Behind the grand public receptions and the glittering Tiffany glass, Arthur waged a silent, courageous battle against a terminal illness, determined to leave his country with an executive office elevated by taste, elegance, and integrity.

6
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

7
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:49.120
[George] Arthur awoke in the executive bedroom, suffering from crushing, paralyzing exhaustion, severe lower back agony, and violent nausea.

8
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:56.400
[George] For weeks, the president had noticed his energy flagging and his boots feeling unusually tight around his swollen ankles.

9
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.320
[George] His personal physician, Dr.

10
00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:58.960
[George] George A.

11
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.520
[George] Loring, was summoned secretly to the second floor of the executive mansion.

12
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:14.640
[George] After examining urine samples, measuring elevated blood pressure, and noting pronounced edema in the president's eyelids and feet, Loring delivered a devastating medical verdict.

13
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] The president was suffering from advanced Bright's disease, a chronic, degenerative inflammation of the kidneys for which 19th-century medicine had no cure.

14
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:35.760
[George] Loring informed Arthur bluntly that his life expectancy was limited to a few short years at best, and that the relentless stress of executive duties would inevitably accelerate his physical decline.

15
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:44.000
[George] In the Gilded Age, there was no constitutional mechanism like the modern 25th Amendment to handle presidential disability.

16
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:54.880
[George] To reveal publicly that the commander-in-chief was dying would trigger catastrophic financial panics on Wall Street and reignite ferocious factional warfare inside the Republican Party.

17
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Arthur looked at his physician with pale, sorrowful eyes and made a solemn decision.

18
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:08.520
[George] He swore Loring to absolute secrecy, commanding him never to disclose the diagnosis to the press or even to members of his cabinet.

19
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:19.000
[George] Arthur resolved to bear his terrible burden in utter silence, fulfilling his constitutional responsibilities with dignity until his term expired or his body surrendered.

20
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:26.680
[George] Confronted with the stark reality of his own mortality, Arthur turned his attention toward transforming his physical surroundings.

21
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:34.040
[George] Throughout the 19th century, the White House had been treated as public property rather than a dignified executive residence.

22
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:41.000
[George] Decades of careless visitors and tobacco-chewing politicians had left the parquet floors permanently stained and ruined.

23
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:52.040
[George] Dusty plush furniture was threadbare and moth-eaten, and the lower corridors smelled of damp rot, stale cabbage, and sewer gas leaking from the nearby Washington Canal.

24
00:02:52.040 --> 00:03:04.600
[George] Arthur, a man of refined European aesthetic sensibilities who appreciated fine art, exquisite tailoring, and quiet order, refused to live in what he scathingly called a badly kept barracks.

25
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.560
[George] In late 1881, Arthur ordered an unprecedented purge of the mansion.

26
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:25.320
[George] Twenty-four massive wagon loads of decaying Victorian clutter, broken chairs, stained carpets, and historic cast-offs dating back to the presidency of John Quincy Adams were hauled out of the cellars and sold at a raucous public auction on the White House lawn.

27
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:38.040
[George] Arthur then hired Louis Comfort Tiffany, the brilliant thirty-four-year-old master of the associated artists and pioneer of the American Art Nouveau movement, to completely reimagine the interior spaces.

28
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:51.760
[George] Tiffany was given a generous budget and total creative license to strip away the dingy, mismatched Victorian wallpaper and replace it with harmony, luminous color, and the finest craftsmanship of the American aesthetic movement.

29
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.320
[George] Tiffany's supreme artistic triumph inside the executive mansion was the legendary stained glass screen in the grand entrance hall.

30
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:13.120
[George] For nearly a century, visitors entering through the north portico could peer directly through the open hallway into the private living quarters of the first family, depriving the president of any semblance of domestic solitude.

31
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:27.520
[George] To provide complete privacy while creating a breathtaking architectural statement, Tiffany constructed a massive, fifty-foot-long screen of shimmering, opalescent favrel glass that spanned the corridor from the marble floor to the high ceiling.

32
00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.600
[George] The screen was an extraordinary technical and aesthetic marvel.

33
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:46.080
[George] Tiffany embedded thousands of hand-blown, iridescent glass tiles in rich shades of amber, ruby, silver, and mother of pearl, crowned by stylized American eagles and flags interwoven into delicate classical arabesques.

34
00:04:46.080 --> 00:05:01.760
[George] When late afternoon sunlight poured through the northern entrance, or when the gas chandeliers were illuminated in the evening, the screen glowed with the fiery brilliance of an enormous jewel box, casting dancing patterns of shimmering colored light across the marble columns.

35
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.240
[George] In the red room, Tiffany painted the walls an opulent Pompeian red accented with real gold leaf.

36
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:15.360
[George] In the blue room, he installed delicate Robinsegg blue surfaces decorated with hand stenciled silver stars.

37
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:22.240
[George] Arthur had created a sacred haven of beauty where he could find solace during his darkest hours of physical agony.

38
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] As Arthur's kidney disease progressed, his physical stamina deteriorated markedly, forcing him to adapt his daily routine.

39
00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:44.280
[George] He rarely appeared in the executive offices before ten o'clock in the morning, leading cynical Washington journalists to pen scathing columns mocking him as a lazy Manhattan dandy who slept until noon and neglected the affairs of state.

40
00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:54.520
[George] In truth, Arthur had spent the entire night wrestling with blinding uremic headaches, intense nausea, and feverish chills that made restful sleep impossible.

41
00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:05.800
[George] To escape the suffocating emotional confinement of the White House and the humid swamp air of Washington, Arthur frequently ordered his private carriage hitched at two or three o'clock in the morning.

42
00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:20.680
[George] Accompanied only by a trusted personal friend or a quiet military aide, Arthur would walk for hours through the deserted, gas-lit streets of Washington, breathing in the cool night breeze and gazing up at the unfinished obelisk of the Washington Monument.

43
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:31.320
[George] He often wandered down to the dark banks of the Potomac or sat quietly on the veranda of the soldier's home, reminiscing with gentle sadness about his beloved late wife Nell.

44
00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.680
[George] Despite his immense physical suffering, Arthur never lost his legendary courtesy.

45
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.560
[George] When subordinate clerks made errors, he corrected them with gentle patience.

46
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:58.360
[George] When foreign dignitaries arrived at the mansion, Arthur hosted them with such effortless wit, warmth, and culinary sophistication that European ambassadors reported back to their foreign ministries that President Arthur was the most cultured gentleman in the entire Western Hemisphere.

47
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.840
[George] In April 1883, Arthur suffered a terrifying medical crisis that nearly ended his life.

48
00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:12.680
[George] A sudden, massive internal hemorrhage left him bedridden and ghostly pale for several days, shocking his personal physician.

49
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:23.760
[George] Desperate for a therapeutic change of climate and sea air, Arthur embarked on a naval cruise down to Florida for what was publicly described as a relaxing sport fishing vacation.

50
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:37.600
[George] However, while staying in Kissimmee, Arthur suffered another violent collapse characterized by severe abdominal spasms and dangerous fevers, requiring round-the-clock emergency medical interventions from naval surgeons.

51
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.160
[George] Alert Washington reporters quickly suspected that something was amiss.

52
00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:54.720
[George] Sensational rumors swept through New York and Philadelphia financial markets that President Arthur had died in Florida, and that Secretary of State Frederick Frelinghysen was preparing to assume executive power.

53
00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.280
[George] Stock prices tumbled on Wall Street as uncertainty gripped the nation.

54
00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:05.120
[George] Summoning every reserve of iron willpower he possessed, Arthur forced himself out of his sickbed.

55
00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:16.320
[George] He dressed in an immaculate white linen suit, stepped onto the deck of his steamer, and spent twenty minutes waving and smiling warmly at hundreds of spectators gathered on the Jacksonville wharf.

56
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.520
[George] The public display of vigor immediately calmed the markets and silenced the press rumors.

57
00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.520
[George] But when Arthur stepped back inside his private cabin, he collapsed into unconsciousness on a sofa.

58
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.480
[George] He had averted national panic, but at devastating cost to his failing health.

59
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.800
[George] As the presidential election of 1884 approached, Chester A.

60
00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.480
[George] Arthur found himself in an extraordinary political position.

61
00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:55.360
[George] Many reform-minded Republicans, who had originally dreaded his elevation, were now deeply impressed by his steadfast defense of the civil service, his vetoes of wasteful pork barrel legislation, and his creation of the modern steel Navy.

62
00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.440
[George] Prominent civic leaders urged him to seek a full term in his own right.

63
00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:08.120
[George] Arthur knew with absolute certainty that the physical ordeal of a nationwide campaign and another four years in the White House would be a death sentence.

64
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:17.640
[George] Yet if he publicly withdrew on medical grounds, he would be branded a weakling, and his former machine enemies under Roscoe Conkling would celebrate his surrender.

65
00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.000
[George] Arthur executed a masterstroke of political honor.

66
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:37.480
[George] He allowed his name to be placed into nomination at the Republican National Convention in Chicago to defend his administration's honorable record, but strictly commanded his floor managers not to make deals, trade patronage promises, or twist delegates' arms on his behalf.

67
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.640
[George] When his longtime rival James G.

68
00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:47.000
[George] Blaine secured the nomination on the fourth ballot, Arthur sent him an immediate, gracious telegram of congratulations.

69
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.160
[George] He was quietly overjoyed.

70
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:56.040
[George] He had defended the honor of his presidency without being condemned to another term that his body could not possibly endure.

71
00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.480
[George] On March 4, 1885, Chester A.

72
00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:10.600
[George] Arthur rode down Pennsylvania Avenue alongside Grover Cleveland, smiling warmly as he handed over the reins of government to the first Democratic president elected since the Civil War.

73
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:17.800
[George] That evening, Arthur returned home to his familiar brownstone at 123 Lexington Avenue in Manhattan.

74
00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.440
[George] He knew his remaining days were few.

75
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:29.640
[George] In the final months of his life, Arthur summoned his trusted personal servant and commanded him to bring up large wicker wastebaskets into his private study.

76
00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:40.280
[George] He gathered all of his personal correspondence, political diaries, official records, and letters from party bosses dating back to his days at the New York Custom House.

77
00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:54.880
[George] For hours, Arthur sat beside the roaring hearth as decades of political secrets, confidential bargains, and backroom correspondence from the Gilded Age Machine were methodically fed into the flames until nothing remained but fragile white ash.

78
00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:03.680
[George] He was determined that the sordid mechanics of the spoils era would perish with him, leaving only his public record as president for posterity to judge.

79
00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.080
[George] On the morning of November 18, 1886, Chester A.

80
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:14.480
[George] Arthur suffered a massive cerebral apoplexy and slipped away peacefully at age 57.

81
00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:29.840
[George] When Grover Cleveland and prominent leaders of both parties attended his funeral in New York, the entire nation mourned the accidental president who had conquered his past and demonstrated that the highest office in the land could redeem the human soul.

82
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:30.640
[Rebecca] Chester A.

83
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.120
[Rebecca] Arthur's final years were a testament to the quiet power of personal dignity.

84
00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:42.800
[Rebecca] Facing death in the shadows, he used his remaining hours to beautify the presidency and protect the Republic from instability.

85
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:57.040
[Rebecca] Next week, we begin our two part study of the man who succeeded Arthur, Grover Cleveland, the blunt, immovable reformer from Buffalo who became the only president in American history to serve two non consecutive terms.

86
00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.200
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

